WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.437
A database view can expose quality problems without overwriting the source table.

00:00:05.437 --> 00:00:09.412
The example creates a customer quality issues view.

00:00:09.412 --> 00:00:19.092
It keeps identifying fields such as customer ID, name, email, and phone, and then adds separate true-or-false flags.

00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:19.762
One flag identifies a missing name.

00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:26.912
Another identifies an email that fails the selected shape check.

00:00:26.912 --> 00:00:33.036
Another identifies a phone that does not contain the required number of digits after normalization.

00:00:33.631 --> 00:00:39.013
The second query returns only records where at least one issue flag is true.

00:00:39.013 --> 00:00:45.098
This design is useful because one customer can have more than one problem at the same time.

00:00:45.098 --> 00:00:53.378
The source data stays intact, while the view gives us a reusable quality layer that can support review, reporting,

00:00:53.378 --> 00:00:55.726
or downstream cleaning workflows.
